# {{.project_name}}

The supported delivery path is dev to UAT; follow
[`docs/uat-promotion.md`](docs/uat-promotion.md) after the dev release gate passes.

Governed RAG application generated from the AAI platform template
(`rag-app`, successor of `agentic-rag`): chunking pipeline over governed
source data, a declared retrieval index, traced grounded generation, and a
groundedness release gate.

## Local setup

Before generation, complete the platform's developer onboarding checklist.
Then run the generated preflight and setup:

```bash
python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py
make check
```

Run `make help` for individual development, evaluation, and bundle targets.

Use `python3.12 scripts/setup_dev.py --check-only` to verify tools,
authentication, SDK volume access, and compute-policy visibility without
installing anything. The setup never grants permissions or creates cloud
resources.

Replace every `replace-with-*` value in `aai-platform.yml` before running
a provider integration.

## Development lifecycle

1. Use `notebooks/01_explore.py` for exploration, not production
   orchestration.
2. Put tested application code under `src/app`; the chunking pipeline lives
   in `jobs/build_chunks.py` (Databricks retrieval) and writes a
   CDF-enabled chunk table. It uses stable chunk IDs and Delta `MERGE` so
   reruns update changed chunks and remove deleted ones without full-table
   overwrite. The vector search index itself is human-run
   platform bootstrap over that chunk table (AGENTS.md rule 8) — this
   bundle only references it by name in `aai-platform.yml`. For Azure AI
   Search, index and ingestion are likewise managed outside this bundle.
3. Register prompts with `python scripts/register_prompt.py`. Record the exact
   returned version and set `variables.prompt_version.default` in
   `databricks.yml` to it. Aliases are deployment pointers only; evaluation
   never resolves one.
4. Grow `evals/data/release_cases.json` with your domain's golden and
   failure cases (the seeded cases show the shape). Keep at least 10.
5. After case changes merge, register the reviewed ground truth in Unity
   Catalog with `python scripts/sync_dataset.py`. Evaluation joins the exact
   governed dataset name, ID, digest, and experiment association.
6. Obtain a bounded, non-secret immutable identifier for the knowledge state
   being evaluated, for example `knowledge-v002`. It must identify the
   externally managed document/chunk/index snapshot; never use a timestamp,
   alias, credential, query string, or mutable name as a substitute. Set
   `variables.knowledge_version.default` in `databricks.yml` to that value.
7. Configure the approved `judge-model`, then run the release gate with both
   immutable inputs explicit:

   ```bash
   make evaluate \
     PROMPT_VERSION=<prompt-version> \
     KNOWLEDGE_VERSION=<knowledge-version>
   ```

   This runs routed groundedness, relevance, and safety judges with the
   thresholds in `evals/gate_config.json`. It records the run ID plus the exact
   source state, SDK, prompt URI, dataset, target/judge identities, provider
   configuration digests, RAG limits, and knowledge version. It also persists
   an adopt or reject decision and prints both the evaluation run ID and
   `decision_run_id`. The SDK's strict judge resolver runs before prompt
   loading or RAG-agent construction, so missing, placeholder,
   non-Databricks, or malformed judge endpoints fail locally. After a released
   run passes, add `EVALUATE_ARGS=--update-baseline`.
8. From the same clean commit and unchanged configuration, materialize the v2
   World/Learning/Control evidence printed by the passing gate:

   ```bash
   python scripts/create_release.py \
     --prompt-version <prompt-version> \
     --knowledge-version <knowledge-version> \
     --evaluation-run <run-id>
   ```

   The command fails closed if any join differs, the run is not `FINISHED`,
   the gate did not pass, the dataset is not associated with this experiment,
   or the local tree is dirty/unknown.
9. Promote the prompt with the decision evidence from the same gate:
   `python scripts/promote_prompt.py --version <prompt-version>
   --decision-run-id <decision-run-id> --to validation|production`. The script
   reloads and verifies the persisted decision artifact, tags, gate metrics,
   and exact prompt binding before moving either alias. Production loads only
   the `production` alias.
10. Deploy with `databricks bundle validate -t dev --strict` then
   `databricks bundle deploy -t dev`. The bundle passes the exact prompt and
   knowledge versions to the job; the included main-branch workflow also
   attests the full Git object ID and clean checkout before the remote gate.

Runtime retrieval is bounded: questions cap at 8,000 characters; hybrid
retrieval requests 20 candidates (50 for Azure AI Search), deduplicates and
selects at most eight context documents; each document caps at 4,000
characters and total context at 24,000 characters; generation caps at 1,024
tokens. Security filters are fixed by trusted application construction and
passed to both text and vector portions of hybrid retrieval. Retrieved text
is serialized as untrusted JSON evidence, and citations are emitted only for
documents actually selected into the prompt.

**Index, embeddings, and chunking change together as one release.** To
rebuild: point `search_index`/chunk table at a version-suffixed successor
(e.g. `knowledge_v002`), run the pipeline and the evaluation gate against
it with a new `knowledge_version`, then switch `aai-platform.yml` to the new
index and keep the previous one for rollback. The version is a join key, not
a replacement for the governed source, chunk, or index controls.

Pull-request CI runs only the deterministic checks
(`evals/offline_checks.py`) — it never holds credentials. The platform team
must onboard this repository (main-branch federated credential, service
principal, constrained compute, non-secret repository variables, and — for
Databricks retrieval — a platform-provisioned vector search endpoint)
before the deploy workflow is enabled.
